.TH SNATCHER 1 2026-05-30 snatcher "Snatcher Manual"
.SH NAME
snatcher \- Transmission BitTorrent CLI client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snatcher
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I command
.RI [ "command options" ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B snatcher
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snatcher
is a modern CLI for managing BitTorrent clients, Transmission first.
It is built so that anything the (forthcoming) TUI can do is also scriptable
from the command line.
.PP
Commands speak to a single configured Transmission server over its RPC
interface. The non-secret connection settings (server URL and username) are
stored in a JSON config file; the password is stored only in the operating
system keyring, never on disk. Run
.B snatcher auth set
once to configure a server before using the other commands.
.PP
Every command that produces output renders in one of three modes with a fixed
precedence:
.BR \-\-jq " \(-> " \-\-json " \(-> plaintext."
The JSON field names are a stable scripting contract. Human and plaintext
output is sanitized of terminal control sequences (server-derived names,
labels, errors, and paths are untrusted); JSON and jq output is never
sanitized, since it is a structured contract the consumer handles.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global options
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit. Valid on
.B snatcher
itself.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help for
.B snatcher
or any subcommand and exit.
.SS Common options
These options are accepted by every command that talks to the server
.RB ( auth\ show ", " auth\ test ", " stats ", " settings )
and by all the
.B torrent
subcommands.
The
.B api
command also accepts
.B \-\-config
and
.B \-\-jq
but uses
.B \-\-raw
in place of
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIpath\fR
Read configuration from
.I path
instead of the default location (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of plaintext.
.TP
.BR \-\-jq " " \fIexpr\fR
Filter JSON output through the jq expression
.IR expr .
Implies JSON and takes precedence over
.BR \-\-json .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "auth"
Manage server authentication. Configure and verify the connection to your
Transmission server. The
.B auth
command has four subcommands.
.TP
.B snatcher auth set \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Store the server URL and username in the config file and the password in the
OS keyring. The password is read from a hidden, non-echoing prompt (with
confirmation) or, for scripting, from standard input with
.BR \-\-password\-stdin .
An empty password is a valid choice (a server with authentication disabled)
and stores no secret. Rekeying to a different URL deletes the old URL's keyring
entry. Options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-url " " \fIurl\fR
Transmission RPC URL, e.g.\&
.IR http://host:9091/transmission/rpc .
Required. Must be an absolute http or https URL with no embedded credentials.
.TP
.BR \-\-username " " \fIname\fR
Username for HTTP Basic Auth.
.TP
.B \-\-password\-stdin
Read the password from standard input instead of prompting.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher auth show \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show the current server URL, username, resolved config path, and whether a
keyring secret exists. The secret value itself is never revealed. Accepts
.BR \-\-config " and " \-\-json .
.TP
.B snatcher auth test \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Dial the configured server and perform a real round-trip, reporting the
connection status, server version, negotiated protocol, and RPC semver. Exits
non-zero on failure. Accepts
.BR \-\-config " and " \-\-json .
.TP
.B snatcher auth remove \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Delete the keyring secret and clear the credentials from the config file.
Accepts
.BR \-\-config .
.SS "stats"
.B snatcher stats
.RI [ options ]
.br
Show session statistics: torrent counts, current download and upload speeds,
and cumulative and current-session transfer totals. Accepts the common options
only.
.SS "settings"
.B snatcher settings
.RI [ options ]
.br
Manage server (session) settings. With no subcommand, list every session
setting as a
.BR CATEGORY " " KEY " " VALUE
table (the same terminal/piped contract as
.BR list ),
filterable by category and key. The
.B settings
command has three subcommands:
.BR get ", " set ", and " reset .
Speed limits
.RB ( speed\-limit\-down ", " speed\-limit\-up ", " alt\-speed\-down ", " alt\-speed\-up )
are in KB/s; the alt-speed schedule times
.RB ( alt\-speed\-time\-begin ", " alt\-speed\-time\-end )
are minutes after midnight (e.g.\& 540 is 09:00). Besides the common options,
the bare command accepts:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-category " " \fIname\fR
Show only settings in one category. One of
.BR download ", " network ", " bandwidth ", " queue .
.TP
.BR \-\-search " " \fItext\fR
Show only settings whose key contains
.I text
(case-insensitive).
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher settings get \fIkey\fR
Print a single setting's current value. Plaintext emits the bare value so it
composes in scripts (e.g.\&
.BR "port=$(snatcher settings get peer-port)" );
.B \-\-json
emits a single
.RB { key ", " category ", " value }
object. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher settings set \fIkey value\fR \fR[\fIkey value\fR]...
Set one or more settings, given as key/value pairs. All pairs are validated
before any write, so an invalid pair aborts the whole command with no partial
change; afterwards the server's actual values are read back and reported.
Read-only settings (e.g.\&
.BR blocklist\-size )
are rejected. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher settings reset \fIkey\fR
Restore a single setting to its Transmission default. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.SS "port"
.B snatcher port
.RI [ options ]
.br
View, change, and test the BitTorrent peer/listening port \(em a discoverable
wrapper over the peer-port session settings. With no subcommand it prints help.
.TP
.B snatcher port get \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show the current peer port alongside the random-on-start and port-forwarding
flags read from the live session. Plaintext is an aligned
.RI Label : " value"
block;
.B \-\-json
emits a stable
.RB { port ", " random_on_start ", " forwarding_enabled }
object. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher port set \fR[\fIport\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
Set the peer port and/or related flags, then re-read the session and report the
resulting state in the same shape as
.BR "port get" .
A
.I port
must be an integer in
.RB [ 1 ", " 65535 ];
giving one also disables
.B random_on_start
so the port sticks across restarts, unless
.B \-\-random
is passed explicitly. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-random\fR[=\fIbool\fR]
Randomize the peer port on each server start. A bare
.B \-\-random
means true; an explicit flag overrides the auto-disable triggered by a
.I port
argument.
.TP
.B \-\-forwarding\fR[=\fIbool\fR]
Enable or disable port forwarding (UPnP/NAT-PMP).
.RE
.IP
With neither a
.I port
nor any flag, it returns a "nothing to set" error. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher port test \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Test whether the currently configured peer port is reachable from outside (it
takes no
.I port
argument). Reports
.B open
or
.B closed
in plaintext and emits an
.RB { open ", " ip_protocol }
object under
.BR \-\-json
(\c
.B ip_protocol
is reported only by Transmission's JSON-RPC 2.0 scheme and omitted otherwise).
Exits 0 when the port is open and non-zero when it is closed, while still
writing the structured result to stdout; a connection or authentication failure
is a distinct non-zero exit that renders no result. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.SS "torrent"
Manage individual torrents. All torrent operations live under the
.B torrent
command group:
.BR info ", " list ", " start ", " stop ", " verify ", " reannounce ", " move ", " remove ", and " upload .
The mutation subcommands
.RB ( start ", " stop ", " verify ", " reannounce ", " remove )
select torrents by numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all ;
at least one selector is required, so a bare invocation is an error, not a
no-op. Every subcommand accepts the common options.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent info \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
Show the full set of fields for a single torrent, identified by its numeric id
or 40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one selector is required. On a terminal
the output is an aligned
.IR key ": " value
block; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is
.IR key <TAB> value
lines with no header \(em a stable scripting contract. With
.B \-\-json
the torrent is emitted as a single JSON object (not an array), and
.B \-\-jq
filters that object. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated fields to show in the plaintext output, in the given order.
When omitted the full field set is shown. Available fields:
.BR id ", " hash ", " name ", " status ", " done ", " size ", " down ,
.BR up ", " ratio ", " eta ", " peers ", " added ", " download_dir ,
.BR error ", " labels .
This affects plaintext only:
.B \-\-json
always emits the full object and
.B \-\-jq
selects fields itself.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent list \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
.RB ( ls )
List torrents. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a header; when
piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-filter " " \fIstatus\fR
Filter by status (default
.BR all ).
One of
.BR all ", " active ", " downloading ", " seeding ", " stopped ,
.BR verifying ", " queued\-to\-verify ", " queued\-to\-download ,
.BR queued\-to\-seed ", " error .
.TP
.BR \-\-search " " \fItext\fR
Show only torrents whose name contains
.I text
(case-insensitive).
.TP
.BR \-\-sort " " \fIfield\fR
Sort by field (default
.BR queue ).
One of
.BR queue ", " name ", " added ", " size ", " progress ", " ratio ", " speed .
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show. The default set is
.BR id , name , status , done , size , down , up , ratio , eta .
Available columns:
.BR id ", " hash ", " name ", " status ", " done ", " size ", " down ,
.BR up ", " ratio ", " eta ", " peers ", " added ", " download_dir ,
.BR error ", " labels .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent start \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Start or resume the selected torrents. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Start every torrent.
.TP
.B \-\-now
Bypass the queue and start immediately.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent stop \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Stop (pause) the selected torrents. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Stop every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent verify \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Verify local torrent data by queueing a re-hash of the downloaded files against
the torrent metadata. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Verify every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent reannounce \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Reannounce the selected torrents to their trackers immediately, rather than
waiting for the next scheduled announce interval. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Reannounce every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent move \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR \fIlocation\fR
.RB ( mv )
Move a single torrent's download location. Select the torrent by numeric id or
40-character SHA1 hash. By default the existing files are relocated to
.IR location .
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-in\-place
Repoint Transmission at a path where the files already exist (after a manual
move) without relocating them.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue \fR[\fIid | hash\fR]...
Reorder the selected torrents within the download/seed queue. Select torrents by
numeric id or 40-character SHA1 hash; at least one selector is required (there
is no
.B \-\-all
flag, and a bare invocation is an error, not a no-op). Each subcommand accepts
the common options and confirms by naming the affected torrents. With no
subcommand it prints help.
.RS
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue top \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents to the top (front) of the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue up \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents up one position in the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue down \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents down one position in the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue bottom \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents to the bottom (back) of the queue.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent remove \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
.RB ( rm )
Remove the selected torrents from the session. Removal is destructive and
requires confirmation: on a terminal it prompts
.RB [ y/N ];
when standard input is not a terminal it refuses unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Remove every torrent.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-data
Also delete the downloaded local files.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent set \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Change mutable per-torrent fields on the selected torrents. Select torrents by
numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all .
At least one selector and at least one field flag are required; a bare
invocation is an error, not a no-op. Only the fields whose flags are explicitly
given are written, so unspecified fields are left unchanged. Besides the common
options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Set the fields on every torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-priority " " \fIlow | normal | high\fR
Bandwidth priority for the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-queue\-position " " \fIn\fR
Position in the download/seed queue (0-based).
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-limit " " \fIn\fR
Maximum number of peers for the torrent (1 or greater).
.TP
.BR \-\-honors\-session\-limits [= true | false ]
Whether the torrent honors the global session speed limits.
.TP
.BR \-\-sequential\-download [= true | false ]
Download pieces in sequential order (Transmission 4.1+).
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-limit " " \fIkBps | off\fR
Per-torrent download speed limit in kB/s, enabling the limit; the literal
.B off
disables it.
.TP
.BR \-\-upload\-limit " " \fIkBps | off\fR
Per-torrent upload speed limit in kB/s, enabling the limit; the literal
.B off
disables it.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-ratio " " \fIratio | global | unlimited\fR
Per-torrent seed ratio limit. A number sets the limit;
.B global
follows the session default;
.B unlimited
seeds without a ratio limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-idle " " \fIminutes | duration | global | unlimited\fR
Per-torrent idle-seeding limit, as whole minutes (a bare integer or a Go-style
duration such as
.IR 30m ", " 1h ", or " 1h30m );
.B global
follows the session default;
.B unlimited
seeds without an idle limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIname\fR
Label to apply, replacing the torrent's entire label set. May be repeated.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-clear\-labels .
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-labels
Remove all labels from the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-tracker " " \fIurl\fR
Announce URL, replacing the torrent's entire tracker list. May be repeated.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-clear\-trackers .
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-trackers
Remove all trackers from the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-group " " \fIname\fR
Bandwidth group name for the torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent upload \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIfile | url | magnet\fR]...
Add torrents to the server from local
.I .torrent
files, HTTP(S) URLs, or magnet links. Each argument is auto-detected as a
file, URL, or magnet link. With no positional arguments, sources are read from
standard input, one per line; a bare
.B snatcher torrent upload
with no sources and no piped input is an error. Multiple sources are processed
continue-on-error: a failure adding one source is reported but does not stop
the rest, and the command exits non-zero if any source failed. Besides the
common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-start
Start torrents immediately instead of adding them paused.
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-dir " " \fIpath\fR
Server-side destination directory.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delete
Delete the local
.I .torrent
after a successful, non-duplicate file add.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIlabel\fR
Label to apply to added torrents. May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress per-source confirmation lines (plaintext only; never alters JSON/jq
output).
.RE
.SS "api"
.B snatcher api
.RI [ options "] " method
.br
Power-user escape hatch, modeled on
.BR "gh api" ,
that issues an arbitrary Transmission RPC
.I method
(canonical kebab-case, e.g.\&
.IR session\-get ", " torrent\-get ", " queue\-move\-top )
and prints a normalized response envelope that is identical whether the server
speaks the bespoke dialect (Transmission 4.0 and earlier) or JSON-RPC 2.0
(4.1 and later). snatcher translates the method name only; it never rewrites
argument keys or values, which are not portable across dialect versions. An
RPC-level failure still prints the envelope, then exits non-zero. Options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-field " " \fIkey=value\fR
Add a type-inferred argument field:
.BR true ", " false ", and " null
become JSON literals, numbers become JSON numbers,
.IR @file " or " @\-
read a file or standard input as a string, and anything else is a string. The
.I key:=value
form parses the value as raw JSON. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-raw\-field " " \fIkey=value\fR
Add a string argument field; the value is always a literal string. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-input " " \fIfile\fR
Supply the entire arguments object as raw JSON from
.IR file ,
or
.B \-
for standard input. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-field " and " \-\-raw\-field .
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Print the server's exact response bytes, bypassing normalization. Mutually
exclusive with
.BR \-\-jq .
.RE
.PP
The
.B api
command also accepts
.B \-\-config
and
.BR \-\-jq ;
it has no
.B \-\-json
flag, since the normalized envelope is already JSON.
.SS "version, completion, help"
.B snatcher version
prints version information.
.B snatcher completion
generates a shell completion script for bash, zsh, fish, or PowerShell.
.B snatcher help
.RI [ command ]
shows help for any command.
.SH OUTPUT
When a command can emit structured output, the render mode is chosen with this
precedence:
.BR \-\-jq " (highest), then " \-\-json ", then plaintext (default)."
The
.B json
field tags on the underlying types are a stable scripting surface; treat them
as an API. Human and plaintext output passes server-derived strings through a
terminal sanitizer; JSON and jq output does not, as it is a machine contract.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred: the server was unreachable, authentication failed, the
selectors matched no torrents, a confirmation was refused, or at least one
.B upload
source failed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SNATCHER_SERVER_URL
Overrides the
.B server_url
from the config file.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_USERNAME
Overrides the
.B username
from the config file.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, forces
.B list
into the piped, tab-separated, header-less contract even on a terminal.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
When set, the default config path is
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snatcher/config.json .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snatcher/config.json
Non-secret configuration (JSON keys
.B server_url
and
.BR username ),
created with mode 0600. Falls back to
.I ~/.config/snatcher/config.json
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
.PP
The server password is not stored in any file. It lives only in the operating
system keyring (macOS Keychain or the Linux Secret Service), under the service
name
.B snatcher
keyed by the server URL.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Configure and verify a server"
.EX
# point snatcher at your Transmission RPC endpoint (prompts for a password)
$ snatcher auth set \-\-url http://localhost:9091/transmission/rpc \-\-username admin

# read the password from stdin instead of a prompt (scripting)
$ printf '%s' "$TRANSMISSION_PASSWORD" | snatcher auth set \(rs
    \-\-url http://localhost:9091/transmission/rpc \-\-username admin \-\-password\-stdin

# confirm the connection works
$ snatcher auth test
.EE
.SS "List and filter torrents"
.EX
# seeding torrents, highest ratio first
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-filter seeding \-\-sort ratio \-\-reverse

# pick the columns you care about
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-fields id,name,ratio,up

# search by name
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-search ubuntu

# names of torrents that are not finished, via jq
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-jq '.[] | select(.done < 100) | .name'
.EE
.SS "Start, stop, and remove torrents"
.EX
# start three torrents by id, jumping the queue
$ snatcher torrent start 1 2 3 \-\-now

# pause everything
$ snatcher torrent stop \-\-all

# remove a torrent and its data without prompting
$ snatcher torrent remove 5 \-\-delete\-data \-\-yes
.EE
.SS "Add torrents"
.EX
# add a file and a magnet link, label them, and start immediately
$ snatcher torrent upload ubuntu.iso.torrent 'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:...' \(rs
    \-\-label linux \-\-label iso \-\-start

# add every source listed in a file (one per line)
$ snatcher torrent upload < sources.txt
.EE
.SS "Session statistics"
.EX
$ snatcher stats
.EE
.SS "Inspect and change settings"
.EX
# list the bandwidth settings
$ snatcher settings \-\-category bandwidth

# read one value (bare value, script-friendly)
$ snatcher settings get peer\-port

# set the download speed limit to 500 KB/s and enable it
$ snatcher settings set speed\-limit\-down 500 speed\-limit\-down\-enabled true

# restore a setting to its default
$ snatcher settings reset peer\-port
.EE
.SS "Raw RPC calls"
.EX
# read the whole session, pick one field with jq
$ snatcher api session\-get \-\-jq '.arguments.version'

# call torrent-get with typed fields
$ snatcher api torrent\-get \-F ids:=1 \-F fields:='["id","name"]' \(rs
    \-\-jq '.arguments.torrents'
.EE
.SH "REPORTING BUGS"
Report bugs at https://github.com/itspriddle/snatcher/issues
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR transmission\-daemon (1),
.BR jq (1)
